  "fidelity_caveat": "Hamm's explicit instruction: high correlation here is NOT sufficient on its own. The downstream calibration (empirical_fpr) and power numbers in `table` are the actual acceptance criterion for whether a Zhat tier is usable -- a good fidelity number must not be allowed to stand in for or overshadow a bad downstream calibration number.",
  "notes": [],
  "interpretation": {
    "decisive_finding": "NO observable Zhat tier (Zhat-0/1/2) approaches oracle-level calibration when the confound includes shared GAIN state. On gain_null (jitter=0, amp_gain=0.6, beta=0), empirical FPR (delta>0 and >0.05) is 1.00 for Zhat-0, Zhat-1, AND Zhat-2 (delta_mean ~0.54-0.60), versus 0.00 for oracle (delta_mean=-0.0008). combined_null shows the same pattern: FPR=1.00 for all three observable tiers (delta_mean ~0.41-0.46) vs 0.00 for oracle (delta_mean=+0.0024). This is a catastrophic, not marginal, failure -- the 'safe' amplitude covariate (Zhat-1/2's ostensible gain proxy, estimate_amplitude_covariate on P's pre-event baseline window) removes essentially none of the spurious Delta_LFP. By contrast, the TIMING confound alone (timing_null: jitter=8, gain=0, beta=0) is handled far better -- FPR=0.10 (Zhat-0/1), 0.00 (Zhat-2), 0.00 (oracle), delta_mean only ~0.007-0.012 across all tiers, an order of magnitude smaller than the gain-driven false positive.",
    "why_amplitude_fails_on_gain": "Nuisance-estimation fidelity (combined_null pool, n=6000 trials): amplitude (rescaled by baseline_amp=0.15) vs true_gain has Pearson r=+0.28, bias=+0.037, RMSE=1.96 -- a weak, noisy proxy. timing_hat vs true_jitter fares similarly weakly (r=+0.35 in timing_null, r=+0.24 in combined_null) yet the downstream timing-confound calibration is far better than the gain-confound calibration. This means proxy correlation strength alone does NOT predict downstream calibration success -- per Hamm's instruction, fidelity numbers here must not be read as a calibration substitute, and this benchmark's own results are the direct demonstration of why: a ~r=0.28-0.35 proxy is apparently sufficient to null a linear timing confound reasonably well but is NOT remotely sufficient to null the gain confound's much larger-magnitude spurious Delta_LFP. The mechanism is likely that gain (a multiplicative AR(1)-driven scale on both P and R's kernel amplitude) has a much stronger and more persistent (autocorrelated across trials via amp_phi=0.95) effect on the lag_features/outcome relationship than a per-trial additive timing jitter does, so the SAME absolute proxy noise leaves proportionally far more unremoved confound variance when the confound is gain-driven.",
    "zhat2_vs_zhat1": "Zhat-2 (adding timing_hat to Zhat-1) does NOT help on any gain-dominated scenario, and measurably HURTS power on the gain-only and combined positive controls with 20 seeds (not just the 5-seed smoke test): gain_PC2 power drops from 0.10 (Zhat-1) to 0.00 (Zhat-2); combined_PC2 power drops from 0.25 (Zhat-1) to 0.00 (Zhat-2). On timing_PC2, where jitter genuinely varies, Zhat-2 still reaches power=1.00 (same as Zhat-1) but its delta_mean drops from 0.8299 to 0.6555 -- part of the true coupling signal is being absorbed into M2 once timing_hat enters, even though power survives there. On the gain-only scenarios (jitter_sd_ms=0.0, so timing_hat is estimating pure noise with nothing real to find), adding this noisy near-zero-information column to M2 still measurably shrinks the M3-M2 gap and directly costs power. This settles the question the smoke test raised: Zhat-2 is not a net improvement over Zhat-1 in this benchmark, and can actively cost power. Zhat-1 (own_history + amplitude only) is the better-calibrated observable tier of the two, though both fail equally badly on the gain-confound FPR.",
    "direction_and_localization": "Direction recovery, exact-interval, and near-interval recovery are all 1.00 (20/20 seeds) for every positive-control scenario (timing_PC2, gain_PC2, combined_PC2, and both directions of P_to_R_only/R_to_P_only/bidirectional) -- this diagnostic is computed via integrated_lag_coefficients on the full-data M3 fit and is tier-invariant by construction (it does not depend on which Zhat tier produced the M2/M3 Delta_LFP table), so it cannot itself distinguish tiers, but it does establish that WHEN detection occurs, the localization/direction machinery is not the degraded component -- calibration is.",
    "scenarios_7_to_9_timing_only_clean": "Scenarios 7-9 (bidirectional generator) use jitter_sd_ms=8.0, amp_gain=0.0 -- i.e. only the timing confound is active, no gain confound. Consistent with timing_null's relatively benign FPR profile, all three observable tiers correctly discriminate direction here: P_to_R_only shows power=1.00 in the true (P->R) direction and FPR<=0.05 in the reverse direction for every tier including Zhat-0; R_to_P_only mirrors this in the opposite direction; bidirectional shows power=1.00 both ways. A = delta_p_to_r - delta_r_to_p correctly signs positive for P_to_R_only (+0.43 to +0.58 across tiers) and negative for R_to_P_only (-0.66 to -0.68), tracking oracle closely (oracle A=+0.5391 and -0.6866 respectively). This means the Zhat bridge degradation found above is SPECIFIC to the gain confound, not a general property of observable-proxy conditioning -- it does not appear when only timing jitter is the shared nuisance.",
    "verdict": "No observable Zhat tier is a usable real-data substitute for the oracle when a gain-like shared confound may be present, because Zhat-0/1/2 are statistically indistinguishable from each other and from an uncorrected model on gain_null/combined_null (FPR=1.00 for all three, delta magnitudes ~0.4-0.6 -- comparable in size to genuine positive-control effects, ~0.5-0.6), while oracle correctly nulls it (FPR=0.00). Zhat-0/1/2 ARE usable when only timing jitter varies (timing_null FPR<=0.10, scenarios 7-9 all show clean direction/power discrimination close to oracle). The amplitude covariate as currently constructed (single pre-event baseline-window mean) is not a sufficient gain proxy for this identification problem; a better real-data nuisance model needs either a smoothed/cross-trial gain estimator with substantially higher fidelity than r~0.28, or an explicit acknowledgment that gain-like confounds cannot currently be safely conditioned away with observable data alone. This is the single most important result of this benchmark and should gate any real-data claim of P->R coupling until a materially better gain proxy is validated or gain-confound magnitude is bounded on real data."
  }
}